{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Is my father still alive?</b> At the beginning of his speech he asked them about his father: 'Is my father still alive?' <b>And his brothers could not answer him, for they were dismayed before him.</b> Trembling seized them and they fell silent, because they had sold him into slavery and now saw him like a king; and further, they were dismayed because they feared for their lives, lest he repay them with evil, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 13:8): ״ונבהלו צירים וחבלים יאחזון״." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> Joseph said to them: Did you not say of this one's brother that he is dead? I will call him and he will come to me. And he would call out, 'Joseph son of Jacob, come to me,' and they would look in all four directions of the house. He said to them: Why are you looking? I am Joseph your brother. Immediately their souls flew away and his brothers could not answer him, for they were dismayed before him. Rabbi Yochanan said: Woe to us from the Day of Judgment, woe to us from the Day of Reproof. For if Joseph, when he said to his brothers 'I am Joseph,' — his brothers could not answer him, then when the Holy One, blessed be He, stands in judgment over His creatures, how much more so on one count. As it is said (Isaiah 10:3): ״ומה תעשו ליום פקודה ולשואה ממרחק תבא״, and it is written (Malachi 3:2): ״ומי מכלכל את יום בואו ומי העומד בהראותו״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 45, :notes {0 "The prooftext citation reads 'שעיה' apparently a corruption of 'ישעיה' (Isaiah); the quoted verse is Isaiah 13:8."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:20:21.105585962Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}